// TASK 3: Tracing Controls Across Frameworks
$task_contents[3] = <<<'HTML'
<div class="content-block">
    <h2>The Same Idea, Different Vocabulary</h2>

    <p>Frameworks describe overlapping ideas using different vocabularies. NIST 800-53 calls a concept a control with a specific identifier. ISO 27001 calls the same idea an Annex A clause. CIS Controls groups it under a numbered Control. The underlying security concept rarely changes. Only the label does.</p>

    <p>This is similar to the same law being translated into different languages. The meaning stays constant even though the wording, numbering, and structure differ between each translation.</p>

    <h3>Crosswalk Example</h3>
    <table class="content-table">
        <thead>
            <tr>
                <th>Control Concept</th>
                <th>NIST 800-53</th>
                <th>ISO 27001</th>
                <th>CIS Controls</th>
            </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
            <tr>
                <td>Least Privilege</td>
                <td>AC-6</td>
                <td>A.5.15</td>
                <td>Control 6</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td>Audit Logging</td>
                <td>AU-2</td>
                <td>A.8.15</td>
                <td>Control 8</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td>Encryption at Rest</td>
                <td>SC-28</td>
                <td>A.8.24</td>
                <td>Control 3</td>
            </tr>
        </tbody>
    </table>

    <div class="info-box">
        <h4><i class="fas fa-info-circle"></i> Why Crosswalking Matters</h4>
        <p>Organizations frequently must satisfy multiple frameworks at once. A customer contract might require ISO 27001 certification while a federal contract requires NIST 800-53 compliance. A crosswalk lets one control satisfy both obligations instead of duplicating the work twice.</p>
    </div>
</div>
HTML;

