$task_contents[4] = <<<'HTML'
<div class="content-block">
    <h2>One Source of Truth</h2>

    <p>The controls matrix is the artifact that ties requirements, controls, framework references, and evidence together into a single source of truth. It typically has columns for the requirement, the control, its framework references, its implementation status, and the location of its evidence.</p>

    <h3>Example Matrix Row</h3>
    <table class="content-table">
        <thead>
            <tr>
                <th>Requirement</th>
                <th>Control</th>
                <th>Framework Reference</th>
                <th>Status</th>
                <th>Evidence Location</th>
            </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
            <tr>
                <td>Restrict access to job related resources only</td>
                <td>Role based access control</td>
                <td>NIST 800-53 AC-6, CIS Control 6</td>
                <td>Implemented</td>
                <td>IAM policy export, Q3 access review</td>
            </tr>
        </tbody>
    </table>

    <div class="success-box">
        <h4><i class="fas fa-lightbulb"></i> Why This Pays Off</h4>
        <p>A well maintained matrix gives an organization instant audit readiness, since every question an auditor asks can be answered by pointing to a row, and immediate gap visibility, since any requirement without a completed row is a finding waiting to happen.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="info-box">
        <h4><i class="fas fa-info-circle"></i> A Living Document</h4>
        <p>Every time a control changes, a system is decommissioned, or a new framework obligation appears, the matrix must be updated. A matrix that was accurate a year ago and never touched since is functionally useless during an audit.</p>
    </div>
</div>
HTML;

// TASK 5: Evidence and Lifecycle Ownership
$task_contents[5] = <<<'HTML'
<div class="content-block">
    <h2>Proof That Follows the System</h2>

    <p>Evidence is anything that proves a control is actually working: configuration exports, log samples, policy documents, test results, screenshots of settings, or signed review reports. Evidence only counts if it is current, specific to the system in question, and clearly tied to the control it supports.</p>

    <p>Ownership of that evidence follows the system through its entire lifecycle. A security engineer's responsibility does not end once a control is implemented. It continues through operation and into every audit the system faces.</p>

    <h3>Evidence Responsibility by Stage</h3>
    <table class="content-table">
        <thead>
            <tr>
                <th>Lifecycle Stage</th>
                <th>Security Engineer's Evidence Responsibility</th>
            </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
            <tr>
                <td>Design</td>
                <td>Define which controls will satisfy each requirement before implementation begins</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td>Implementation</td>
                <td>Configure the control and capture baseline evidence that it was deployed correctly</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td>Operation</td>
                <td>Continuously collect evidence that the control remains active and effective</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td>Audit</td>
                <td>Present current evidence on demand and explain any gaps</td>
            </tr>
        </tbody>
    </table>

